59,3,,status,George,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
60,3,1,talk,Toshio,,"Option B, the Central Study Commons, aligns perfectly with the post-occupancy data. It serves the widest user base.","{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Victoria,,"@Toshio, yes. The booking data shows general rooms are in high demand. This directly addresses that gap.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,George,,I support B. It includes the automation of manual routines that I identified as a critical burden on our staff.,"{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Mizuki,,"@George, I am still wary of the schedule. But the other options have higher risk profiles.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,Option B is the only one that avoids the volatile plant costs. The others are exposed to the market inflation I found.,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Toshio,,Option A is a trap. It serves a narrow group and the post-occupancy ratings for single-wing projects are poor.,"{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Victoria,,"@Toshio, agreed. The timetable impact of A would be severe, and the demand is not there.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,George,,Option A does not address the staffing issue. It just adds more equipment for the same tired technicians to maintain.,"{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Mizuki,,"Option A has a high risk of delay. The exposed work is significant, and the weather data is not in our favor.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"Option A is the most expensive. The plant costs are up a fifth, and there is no reserve for the equipment replacement.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Toshio,,"Option C is a nice-to-have, but it does not solve the core problem of underutilized space.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Victoria,,"@Toshio, correct. The booking data shows no demand for more paths. The demand is for rooms.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,George,,Option C does not help the technicians. It is a cosmetic improvement that does not address the workload.,"{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Mizuki,,"Option C has a moderate delay risk, but the benefit is low. It is not a good use of the capital budget.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"Option C is the cheapest, but the value is low. We are better off investing in space that is actually used.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Toshio,,Option D is a specialist project. The post-occupancy data shows these are rated lowest.,"{""words"":14,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Victoria,,"@Toshio, yes. The booking data shows specialist rooms are idle. We do not need more of them.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,George,,Option D adds to the burden. It requires more technicians for a service that is not in demand.,"{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Mizuki,,"Option D has a high delay risk. The fit-out is complex, and the market for the equipment is volatile.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,"Option D is a financial risk. The equipment is expensive, and the demand is not there to justify the cost.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
